---

<BaseLayout
  title="Adaptive Green Transition in Global Production Networks"
  description="A portfolio case study on ecological transition, complexity economics, agent-based modelling, input-output networks and historical validation."
>
  <section class="project-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Portfolio case study</p>
          <h1>Adaptive Green Transition in Global Production Networks</h1>

          <p class="hero-subtitle">
            A modelling project on ecological transition as productive transformation: how can economies keep developing while reducing the environmental footprint of production fast enough to remain within planetary limits?
          </p>

          <p class="hero-text">
            The policy problem is visible in the data. Countries with high human development often remain far above climate-compatible carbon footprints, while countries within ecological limits often have lower levels of development. The transition challenge is therefore not only to reduce emissions. It is to understand what productive mechanisms could move economies into the space where human development and environmental constraints are compatible.
          </p>

          <p class="hero-text">
            This project approaches that question through complexity economics, economic complexity and agent-based modelling. It treats green transition as a transformation of productive ecosystems: interdependent country-sector systems shaped by capabilities, supplier networks, energy use, lock-in and historical trajectories.
          </p>

          <div class="badge-list">
            {badges.map((badge) => (
              <span class="badge">{badge}</span>
            ))}
          </div>

          <div class="project-links">
            <a href="/projects" class="text-link">Back to projects</a>
            <a href="/" class="text-link">Homepage</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-media">
          <VisualPanel
            title="Development inside planetary limits"
            caption="The transition challenge is to move toward high human development while remaining within climate-compatible carbon footprints."
            showAction={false}
          >
            <img class="hero-plot" src={hdiFootprintPlot} alt="Carbon footprint and Human Development Index by country" />
          </VisualPanel>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-status">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Project outcome</p>
        <h2>A historical diagnostic model designed to become a transition simulator</h2>
        <p>
          The project produced a tested modelling pipeline that turns a theoretical question into data architecture, historical diagnostics, agent-based model components, validation checks and a concrete research roadmap.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="stat-grid">
        {implementationStats.map((stat) => (
          <div class="stat-card">
            <span class="stat-value">{stat.value}</span>
            <span class="stat-label">{stat.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="executive-argument">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Economic theory and policy modelling</p>
      <h2>Why green transition has to be modelled as a production-system problem</h2>

      <p>
        The starting point of this project is the policy problem of ecological transition: how can economic development continue while the environmental footprint of production falls fast enough to remain within planetary limits? This is often discussed through decoupling, meaning the separation of economic activity from environmental pressure. But the important question is not only whether GDP and emissions move apart in observed data. The harder question is transformative: what productive mechanisms can make such a separation possible, and what kind of model can help decision-makers understand where action is feasible?
      </p>

      <p>
        That is why the transition is treated here as a modification of the productive system. Ecological transition does not only mean setting an emissions objective. It means changing the way the economy extracts resources, transforms materials, uses energy, organizes technologies, and connects production across countries and sectors. This is the idea behind the project’s use of “productive ecosystem”: the economy is not a list of sectors reacting separately to policy incentives, but a system of interdependent activities whose structure determines what can change, how fast, and with what side effects.
      </p>

      <p>
        This framing matters because standard equilibrium approaches, including many CGE-style transition models, are not always well suited to this kind of transformation. The green transition is not a marginal adjustment around a stable equilibrium. It is a structural shift in a system where heterogeneous agents interact, adapt, create feedback loops, and modify the structure that then constrains future behaviour. A small change can remain local, but it can also trigger wider reorganization if it crosses a threshold or affects a central node. Complexity economics is useful here because it studies precisely this kind of process: emergence, path dependence, lock-in, non-linear change, and the possibility of multiple transition trajectories rather than one smooth optimal path.
      </p>

      <p>
        Economic complexity then gives a concrete meaning to productive transformation. Countries and sectors cannot move into any cleaner activity simply because it becomes desirable. They move through what they already know how to do: their productive knowledge, technologies, routines, supplier base, infrastructure and adjacent capabilities. A green productive sector is therefore not only a sector with low emissions. It is an activity that can emerge from an existing productive ecosystem, or fail to emerge because the required capabilities are missing or too distant.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="theory-cards-section alternate">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Conceptual toolkit</p>
        <h2>The theoretical tools used in practice</h2>
        <p>
          The model is built from a sequence of theoretical commitments: ecological transition as a policy problem, productive ecosystems as the object of transformation, complexity economics as the dynamics, and economic complexity as the capability constraint.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card-grid four">
        {theoryCards.map((card) => (
          <article class="info-card">
            <h3>{card.title}</h3>
            <p>{card.text}</p>
            <span class="skill-line">{card.skills}</span>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bridge-section">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">From theory to empirical modelling</p>
      <h2>The ABM was built from historical complexity diagnostics</h2>

      <p>
        The model was not built directly from equations. It grew from a sequence of historical diagnostics: mapping green capability, observing movement in transition space, comparing sector trajectories, and identifying large production nodes that do not follow the same path.
      </p>

      <p>
        These diagnostics made the theoretical argument operational. If transition is path-dependent, capability-constrained and networked, then the model needs agents, histories, suppliers, production constraints and validation checks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="phase-space-section alternate">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Phase-space diagnostics</p>
      <h2>Before simulating transition, the project first had to observe movement</h2>

      <p>
        The phase-space work is the bridge between the policy theory and the ABM. Instead of only measuring whether a country-sector is green or brown at one date, it asks where each node is located in a productive transition space and how it moves historically.
      </p>

      <p>
        This matters because a complex adaptive system is not understood only through levels. It is understood through trajectories, direction, speed, clustering and divergence. A country-sector can improve its local emissions profile, remain dependent on brown suppliers, accumulate green capability without immediate emissions gains, or occupy a central position that makes its adjustment more important for the wider system.
      </p>

      <p>
        The phase-space diagnostics therefore prepared the ABM architecture. They showed why the model needed heterogeneous agents, historical states, network-embedded green-ness, sector-specific trajectories and explicit validation against observed movement.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="visual-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="visual-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Historical phase space</p>
        <h2>Green capability and local green-ness rise together, but not smoothly</h2>
        <p>
          The historical trajectory suggests a broad association between green capability and local green-ness, but not a smooth convergence path. This supports the use of a dynamic transition space rather than a static classification of green and brown sectors.
        </p>
        <p class="skills-note">
          Skills used: historical phase-space analysis, economic complexity, transition diagnostics.
        </p>
      </div>

      <VisualPanel
        title="Global transition trajectory"
        caption="Historical movement across green capability and local green-ness, 1995-2016."
        showAction={false}
      >
        <img src={globalPhasePlot} alt="Historical global phase-space trajectory of green capability and local green-ness" />
      </VisualPanel>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="visual-section alternate">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="visual-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Complex dynamics</p>
        <h2>Movement fields show uneven motion across the green-readiness space</h2>
        <p>
          The movement-field diagnostic asks how positions move over time, not only where they are located. It shows why a transition model needs path dependence, local interactions and non-linear movement rather than a single average adjustment rule.
        </p>
        <p class="skills-note">
          Skills used: vector-field diagnostics, non-linear dynamics, visual modelling.
        </p>
      </div>

      <VisualPanel
        title="Historical movement field"
        caption="Observed movement across the green-readiness space."
        showAction={false}
      >
        <img src={vectorFieldPlot} alt="Movement field across green capability and local green-ness" />
      </VisualPanel>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="visual-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="visual-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Sector heterogeneity</p>
        <h2>Sector trajectories reveal distinct capability-constrained transition paths</h2>
        <p>
          Sectors do not share a single transition trajectory. This matters because ecological transition is not just an aggregate emissions problem. It depends on the productive structure of each sector family, its capabilities, its network position and its feasible adjacent transformations.
        </p>
        <p class="skills-note">
          Skills used: sectoral analysis, economic interpretation, capability-constrained modelling.
        </p>
      </div>

      <VisualPanel
        title="Sector transition trajectories"
        caption="Sector-level trajectories across green capability, local green-ness and outgoing network green-ness."
        showAction={false}
      >
        <img src={sectorTrajectoryPlot} alt="Sector trajectories in green transition space" />
      </VisualPanel>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="visual-section alternate">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="visual-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Large nodes and lock-in</p>
        <h2>Large production nodes do not share a single green transition path</h2>
        <p>
          Large country-sector nodes matter because they shape aggregate system dynamics. If central or high-output nodes remain locked into carbon-intensive configurations, aggregate transition can remain slow even when smaller nodes improve.
        </p>
        <p class="skills-note">
          Skills used: production-network interpretation, node-level diagnostics, aggregate-system reasoning.
        </p>
      </div>

      <VisualPanel
        title="Large production nodes"
        caption="Top production nodes across green capability, local green-ness and incoming network green-ness."
        showAction={false}
      >
        <img src={largeNodesPlot} alt="Large production node trajectories in green transition space" />
      </VisualPanel>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="abm-transition-section">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Agent-based modelling</p>
      <h2>Why this becomes an ABM</h2>

      <p>
        The ABM is the modelling translation of the theoretical argument. A production system with heterogeneous agents, network constraints, path dependence, capability limits and local interactions cannot be reduced to a single representative trajectory without losing the mechanisms of interest.
      </p>

      <p>
        ABM v4 therefore represents the global economy as country-sector agents embedded in input-output networks, then tests whether green reorganization can be understood through productive ecosystems, capability constraints, supplier structures, production feasibility and emissions-intensity dynamics. Its purpose is not to claim a ready-made scenario result. Its purpose is to identify which mechanisms are historically defensible, which ones fail, and what must be added before a credible transition-scenario model can be built.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="behavioural-leontief-section alternate">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Input-output economics and ABM design</p>
      <h2>From Leontief accounting to behaviouralized production dynamics</h2>

      <p>
        Input-output economics is the starting point because it represents the economy as an interdependent production system. Each sector produces output, buys intermediate inputs from other sectors, and sells to downstream users. In a multi-country input-output table, this becomes a global production network: each country-sector node is embedded in supplier and buyer relations that shape what it can produce and how shocks can propagate.
      </p>

      <p>
        The basic input-output object is the transaction matrix <strong>T</strong>, where <strong>T</strong><sub>ij</sub> is the value of intermediate inputs sold by supplier sector <strong>i</strong> to buyer sector <strong>j</strong>. From this, the technical-coefficient matrix is defined as:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-grid three">
        <Equation
          label="Technical coefficient"
          tex={String.raw`a_{ij} = \frac{T_{ij}}{X_j}`}
          note="Input from supplier i required per unit of output produced by buyer j."
        />

        <Equation
          label="Static Leontief identity"
          accent={true}
          tex={String.raw`X = AX + Y`}
          note="Gross output equals intermediate production requirements plus final demand."
        />

        <Equation
          label="Classic solution"
          tex={String.raw`X = (I - A)^{-1}Y`}
          note="Output is solved as an equilibrium requirement for a given technical structure and final demand."
        />
      </div>

      <p>
        This is powerful because it makes production interdependence explicit. A change in final demand does not only affect one sector directly. It propagates through the production network via indirect input requirements. This is why input-output economics is central to the project: it provides a rigorous way to represent the productive structure of the economy.
      </p>

      <p>
        But the standard Leontief framework is mostly static. It tells us what level of gross output is required for a given final demand vector and a given technical structure. It does not explain how heterogeneous producers adapt when the structure itself is under transition pressure: when suppliers become constrained, when new capabilities are needed, when energy systems change, or when emissions-intensity reduction requires a different productive configuration.
      </p>

      <p>
        This limitation matters for ecological transition. A green transition is not only a new final-demand vector or a new emissions coefficient. It can require new suppliers, new capabilities, different energy mixes, substitution under friction, investment lags, policy constraints, and the reorganization of production across countries and sectors. A static input-output model can represent interdependence, but it cannot by itself explain adaptive behaviour, lock-in, learning, threshold effects, or the emergence of new productive configurations.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project therefore keeps the Leontief insight but moves it into an agent-based framework. Input-output data define the production environment: technical requirements, supplier-buyer links, production scale and emissions accounting. But country-sector nodes are treated as adaptive agents inside that environment.
      </p>

      <div class="equation-grid two">
        <Equation
          label="ABM input requirement"
          tex={String.raw`R_{ij,t} = a_{ij,t} X^{d}_{j,t}`}
          note="Buyer j requires supplier-specific inputs from i to support desired production."
        />

        <Equation
          label="Behaviouralized production logic"
          accent={true}
          tex={String.raw`X^{r}_{j,t+1} = f(X^{d}_{j,t}, R_{ij,t}, \Omega_{j,t})`}
          note="Realized output emerges from constrained adaptation inside the production network."
        />
      </div>

      <div class="equation-grid one equation-grid-secondary">
        <Equation
          label="Constraint set"
          tex={String.raw`\Omega_{j,t} = \{w_{ij,t}, Cap_{j,t}, GCap_{j,t}, Feas_{j,t}\}`}
          note="The constraint set summarizes supplier weights, capabilities, green capabilities and production feasibility."
        />
      </div>

      <p>
        The model asks whether input requirements can be met through feasible supplier relationships, rather than assuming that the whole system instantly clears through the Leontief inverse. Supplier adjustment is constrained by historical ties, sector proximity, productive ecosystems, supplier reliability, green advantage and substitution friction.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is what I call behaviouralized Leontief logic. The input-output table provides the structural discipline. Complexity economics motivates the adaptive dynamics. Economic complexity defines the capability constraint. The ABM combines them by asking how country-sector nodes adjust locally, and whether those local adjustments produce historically defensible system-level patterns.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="architecture-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">ABM v4 implementation</p>
        <h2>What was built</h2>
        <p>
          ABM v4 is a Python modelling pipeline built around country-sector agents, Eora input-output data, emissions intensity, capability measurement, supplier opportunities and historical validation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <VisualPanel
        title="ABM v4 historical diagnostic stack"
        caption="The project moves from historical data inputs to country-sector agents, production networks, behavioural diagnostic layers, validation and model boundaries."
        showAction={false}
      >
        <img src={architecturePlot} alt="ABM v4 architecture as a historical diagnostic stack" />
      </VisualPanel>

      <div class="card-grid four architecture-cards">
        {architectureCards.map((card) => (
          <article class="info-card">
            <h3>{card.title}</h3>
            <p>{card.text}</p>
            <span class="skill-line">{card.skills}</span>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="visual-section alternate">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="visual-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Core accounting logic</p>
        <h2>Falling emissions is not automatically green transition</h2>
        <p>
          The model separates emissions change into scale effects, intensity effects and interaction effects. This distinction is central. A decline in emissions caused by lower output is not treated as evidence of productive decarbonisation.
        </p>
        <p class="skills-note">
          Skills used: carbon accounting, decomposition logic, transition diagnostics, model interpretation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <VisualPanel
        title="Emissions decomposition"
        caption="The model distinguishes output-scale effects from actual emissions-intensity improvement."
        showAction={false}
      >
        <img src={decompositionPlot} alt="ABM v4 emissions decomposition logic" />
      </VisualPanel>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="visual-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="visual-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Capability measurement</p>
        <h2>Turning missing data into a modelling decision</h2>
        <p>
          The project does not treat every missing capability value as the same problem. Atlas product-space information is used where meaningful. IO-based capability proxies are used where production-network evidence is sufficient. Remaining unavailable cases are explicitly flagged instead of silently filled.
        </p>
        <p class="skills-note">
          Skills used: economic complexity, data quality assessment, feature engineering, transparent imputation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <VisualPanel
        title="Source-aware capability assignment"
        caption="Capability coverage after combining Atlas-observed and IO-imputed information."
        showAction={false}
      >
        <img src={capabilityPlot} alt="Capability source coverage after IO imputation" />
      </VisualPanel>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="validation-section alternate">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Historical validation</p>
        <h2>What the model learned by trying to fail</h2>
        <p>
          The most important part of ABM v4 is not only that a model was implemented. It is that mechanisms were tested, rejected, retained or downgraded based on historical evidence.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card-grid four">
        {validationCards.map((card) => (
          <article class="info-card">
            <h3>{card.title}</h3>
            <p>{card.text}</p>
            <span class="skill-line">{card.skills}</span>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="visual-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="visual-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Model comparison</p>
        <h2>Why ABM v4 retains two historical rules</h2>
        <p>
          The validation did not produce one clean winner. One rule is better for aggregate emissions plausibility, while another gives a cleaner transition-mechanism interpretation. Rather than forcing a false conclusion, ABM v4 keeps both as diagnostic references.
        </p>
        <p class="skills-note">
          Skills used: model comparison, validation metrics, calibration diagnostics, scientific restraint.
        </p>
      </div>

      <VisualPanel
        title="Two-rule validation result"
        caption="ABM v4 retains an aggregate-safe baseline and a transition-mechanism benchmark."
        showAction={false}
      >
        <img src={twoRulePlot} alt="Scorecard explaining why ABM v4 retains two historical rules" />
      </VisualPanel>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="visual-section alternate">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="visual-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Energy-system boundary</p>
        <h2>China electricity exposed what the model still cannot represent</h2>
        <p>
          Electricity is not just another sector. It is infrastructure-heavy, politically regulated, fuel-mix dependent, and essential to the rest of the economy. The China electricity case showed that ABM v4 lacks the energy-system and policy variables needed to explain such nodes as ordinary country-sector agents.
        </p>
        <p class="skills-note">
          Skills used: energy-system diagnosis, anomaly interpretation, historical data audit, model-boundary analysis.
        </p>
      </div>

      <VisualPanel
        title="China electricity as a boundary case"
        caption="Fuel-mix evidence supports the need for explicit energy-system modelling, but does not by itself prove a causal mechanism."
        showAction={false}
      >
        <img src={chinaElectricityPlot} alt="China electricity fuel mix and observed emissions-intensity reduction" />
      </VisualPanel>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="visual-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="visual-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Data quality boundary</p>
        <h2>Q energy mix is useful evidence, but not yet rule-grade data</h2>
        <p>
